---
<p>Bayan has no garbage collector and no lifetimes in the syntax. Values live on the stack, in an allocator, or inside another value. The compiler checks ownership and borrowing.</p>

<h2 id="allocators">Allocators</h2>
<ul>
  <li>Lists, maps and strings that grow allocate from the current allocator. A function that allocates declares <code>uses alloc</code> (see <a href="ref-effects.html">Effects</a>).</li>
  <li><code>Arena()</code> is an allocator that frees everything at once. Create it with <code>var</code>. <code>reset()</code> on a <code>let</code> is BYN2104.</li>
  <li><code>provide allocator: arena { … }</code> makes the arena the allocator for the block.</li>
</ul>
<example name="ref-memory-arena"/>

<h2 id="ownership">Ownership</h2>
<ul>
  <li>A <code>List</code> has one owner. Assigning it to a second name is a compile error (BYN2780) and the help suggests <code>move</code>.</li>
  <li><code>move a</code> hands the value over. Using <code>a</code> afterwards is BYN2781.</li>
  <li>A parameter marked <code>move</code> takes ownership. Call it as <code>f(move a)</code>.</li>
  <li><code>take()</code> moves a field out and leaves it empty. It works on collections, not on structs (BYN2788).</li>
  <li><code>copy()</code> makes an independent owned value.</li>
</ul>
<example name="ref-memory-move"/>
<pre><code class="byn">var a: List&lt;i64&gt; = [1]
a.append(2)
let b = a
print($"{b.length}")</code></pre>
<figure class="diag">
<pre><span class="err">error BYN2780:</span> implicit copy of a unique value: `List&lt;i64&gt;` is unique: it owns its storage
  --&gt; mm-implicit.byn:3:9

<span class="gut">  3 │</span> let b = a
<span class="gut">    │</span>         <span class="hl">^</span>
<span class="gut">    │</span>
  help: hand the value over with `move a`; make an independent copy with `a.copy(to: alloc)`; pass
        it by `ref` to share it instead
<span class="gut">    │</span> 3 │ let b = move a
<span class="gut">    │</span>   │         +++++</pre>
  <figcaption>The compiler’s message for this program.</figcaption>
</figure>
<pre><code class="byn">var a: List&lt;i64&gt; = [1]
a.append(2)
let b = move a
print($"{a.length}")</code></pre>
<figure class="diag">
<pre><span class="err">error BYN2781:</span> `a` is used after it was moved
  --&gt; mm-moved.byn:4:10

<span class="gut">  4 │</span> print($"{a.length}")
<span class="gut">    │</span>          <span class="hl">^ moved on line 3</span>
<span class="gut">    │</span>
  help: assign `a` a new value before using it again, or move a `.copy(to: alloc)` of it instead</pre>
  <figcaption>The compiler’s message for this program.</figcaption>
</figure>
<pre><code class="byn">struct P { i64 x }
var p = P { x: 1 }
p.x = 2
let q = p.take()</code></pre>
<figure class="diag">
<pre><span class="err">error BYN2788:</span> `take()` needs a `List`, `Map`, `StrBuf` or an optional of a unique type, not `P`
  --&gt; mm-take.byn:4:11

<span class="gut">  4 │</span> let q = p.take()
<span class="gut">    │</span>           <span class="hl">^^^^</span>
<span class="gut">    │</span>
